From: Canaan K230 Linux SDK
Subject: [PATCH] live555: let an RTSP server be restarted right away

An RTSP server that had a client connected when it was stopped cannot be
started again for 60 seconds. It dies at startup with

    create rtsp server failed.bind() error (port 8554): Address already in use

This is deliberate upstream behaviour, not a bug in our code. live555 wraps
the listening socket's setup in "NoReuse dummy(env);", which sets
SO_REUSEADDR to 0, unless ALLOW_SERVER_PORT_REUSE or the older
ALLOW_RTSP_SERVER_PORT_REUSE is defined. Neither is defined in this tree.

The part that is easy to get wrong: the listening socket itself never enters
TIME_WAIT. The TIME_WAIT entries belong to the *accepted* client connections,
which have the same local port (8554) and which the server closed first, so
they linger for TCP_TIMEWAIT_LEN. That constant is (60*HZ) in
include/net/tcp.h and no sysctl changes it. This is also why the problem looks
intermittent: with no client ever attached there is nothing in TIME_WAIT and
the restart is instant.

Upstream's stated reason for NoReuse is "Don't use this socket if there's
already a local server using it". On Linux that reason does not hold:
SO_REUSEADDR has never allowed a second socket to bind over a live listener -
that is SO_REUSEPORT, which live555 only sets for UDP (it is behind the
undefined REUSE_FOR_TCP in GroupsockHelper.cpp). So after this patch, starting
a second RTSP server on a port that is already being listened on still fails,
exactly as before. The only thing that changes is the 60-second lockout after
a clean exit.

Note this is NOT the rt-smart change of the same area. rt-smart drops NoReuse
*and* adds SO_LINGER {1,0}, which makes the server send a RST instead of
closing cleanly - that is how it dodges lwIP's stale TIME_WAIT PCBs, and on
Linux it would cut responses short. Only the NoReuse half is carried over.

--- a/liveMedia/GenericMediaServer.cpp	2026-11-25 11:35:08.739587382 +0800
+++ b/liveMedia/GenericMediaServer.cpp	2026-11-25 11:35:18.595214600 +0800
@@ -179,13 +179,26 @@
   int ourSocket = -1;
   
   do {
-    // The following statement is enabled by default.
-    // Don't disable it (by defining ALLOW_SERVER_PORT_REUSE) unless you know what you're doing.
-#if !defined(ALLOW_SERVER_PORT_REUSE) && !defined(ALLOW_RTSP_SERVER_PORT_REUSE)
-    // ALLOW_RTSP_SERVER_PORT_REUSE is for backwards-compatibility #####
-    NoReuse dummy(env); // Don't use this socket if there's already a local server using it
-#endif
-    
+    // Local patch; reapply when upgrading live555.
+    //
+    // Upstream has "NoReuse dummy(env);" here, which turns SO_REUSEADDR off
+    // for the listening socket so that bind() fails if another local server
+    // already holds this port. On Linux that protection is not what keeps it:
+    // SO_REUSEADDR has never let a second socket bind over a live listener
+    // (only SO_REUSEPORT does, and live555 does not set it for TCP), so
+    // removing NoReuse does not make two RTSP servers on one port possible.
+    //
+    // What it does remove is this: after a server that had clients connected
+    // exits, the accepted connections it closed sit in TIME_WAIT holding the
+    // same local port, and without SO_REUSEADDR bind() fails with EADDRINUSE
+    // for TCP_TIMEWAIT_LEN - 60 seconds, not tunable by any sysctl. A camera
+    // that cannot be restarted for a minute after you stop it is a daily
+    // annoyance; two servers on one port is not a thing that happens here.
+    //
+    // Defining ALLOW_SERVER_PORT_REUSE would do the same, but it would have
+    // to be added to COMPILE_OPTS from live555.mk, which is buildroot's file
+    // rather than ours.
+
     ourSocket = setupStreamSocket(env, ourPort, domain, True, True);
         // later fix to support IPv6
     if (ourSocket < 0) break;
